-- Shooter — the extremes. This one exists to be hard on the hardware.
--
--   * a row of enemies at the same y, which is the per-scanline sprite limit's
--     worst case: eight on a NES or a Master System, ten on a Game Boy, and
--     anything past that simply does not draw
--   * fast bullets against thin targets, close to the tunnelling threshold
--   * a magazine of bullets recycled rather than spawned, with more than one of
--     them in the air at a time
--   * two independent level rules driving the same object (the enemy line
--     advances and sways)

start play

scene play

create object ship (width 1 cell, height 1 cell, speed 60vw, sprite hero.svg)
create object shot (width 1 cell, height 1 cell, speed 70vh, sprite shot.svg)
create object alien (width 1 cell, height 1 cell, speed 8vw, sprite alien.svg)

create ship player in play (x centerx, y screenheight - 2)

-- Nine aliens on one row. On a Game Boy that is nine of the ten sprites the
-- hardware will draw on that scanline; send a shot through the line and the
-- eleventh vanishes. Three shots make that a certainty rather than a risk, which
-- is the point of keeping this fixture: the simulator reports the peak as it
-- runs, and a budget nobody can exceed is a budget nobody reads.
create alien a1 in play (x 4vw, y 3, direction east)
create alien a2 in play (x 14vw, y 3, direction east)
create alien a3 in play (x 24vw, y 3, direction east)
create alien a4 in play (x 34vw, y 3, direction east)
create alien a5 in play (x 44vw, y 3, direction east)
create alien a6 in play (x 54vw, y 3, direction east)
create alien a7 in play (x 64vw, y 3, direction east)
create alien a8 in play (x 74vw, y 3, direction east)
create alien a9 in play (x 84vw, y 3, direction east)

-- The magazine: three shots, parked and inert until one is loaded. `visible 0`
-- is what "in the rack" means — not drawn, not collided with, not moved — so a
-- spent shot needs no lifecycle concept to go back in it.
create shot s1 in play (x centerx, y screenheight - 3, visible 0, direction north)
create shot s2 in play (x centerx, y screenheight - 3, visible 0, direction north)
create shot s3 in play (x centerx, y screenheight - 3, visible 0, direction north)

-- One press, one shot — whichever is free. Without this the three firing rules
-- below would each see a free shot on the same button edge and empty the whole
-- magazine at once. It is cleared with the level rules and read by the edge
-- rules, which is the same tick-order trick the platformer's `footing` uses.
create number fired in play (value 0, visible 0)

create text scoretag in play (x 1, y 1, text "hits:")
create number score in play (value 0, x 7, y 1)

control player left (xdirection -1) on hold
control player right (xdirection 1) on hold

when always in play then fired.value as 0

when player hits screenleft, screenright then xdirection as 0

-- Firing. Three rules of the same shape, and the first one whose shot is free
-- takes the press: a comparison is 1 or 0, so multiplying two of them is "both",
-- which is all a guard needs when the language has no `and`.
--
-- The `if` is what makes this a *reload* rather than a restart: a shot already
-- in flight is not in the rack, so pressing again launches the next one instead
-- of teleporting the first back to the ship.
when a pressed if (s1.visible = 0) * (fired.value = 0) > 0 then (s1.visible, s1.x, s1.y, fired.value) as (1, player.x, player.y - 1, 1)
when a pressed if (s2.visible = 0) * (fired.value = 0) > 0 then (s2.visible, s2.x, s2.y, fired.value) as (1, player.x, player.y - 1, 1)
when a pressed if (s3.visible = 0) * (fired.value = 0) > 0 then (s3.visible, s3.x, s3.y, fired.value) as (1, player.x, player.y - 1, 1)

-- A shot that reaches the top goes back in the rack. Naming the class rather
-- than each shot means one rule for the whole magazine, with the shot that
-- arrived bound as its subject.
when shot hits screentop then visible as 0

when shot hits alien then (alien.visible, shot.visible, score.value) as (0, 0, score.value + 1)

-- The line sways by bouncing off the walls. A `hits` rule can target the *class*
-- — every alien at once — because collision binds the object that collided. A
-- level rule cannot: `when always (alien.xdirection)` has no subject to bind, so
-- driving a whole class from a condition is not expressible today. Another
-- finding this file is here to produce.
when alien hits screenleft, screenright then xdirection as flip

when score.value reaches 9 in play then scene as cleared

scene cleared

create text swept in cleared (x centerx - 6, y centery, text "line cleared")

when a pressed in cleared then scene as play
